CHANGE 1 -- arena reserves. `TraceStore::absorb` appends with insert() and no reserve. Two merge
loops (12 band stores after banded detection; 48 chunk stores in splitIntegerTraces) absorb into a
destination that was just swapped empty, so it regrew geometrically. I prefix-sum the source arena
sizes and reserve `inten` and, if any source has them, `bins`. Question: is the `any_bins` condition
right given absorb() does `bins.resize(base, 0u)` before inserting? Can reserving bins to the inten
total ever be too small? Can the reserve itself raise peak memory rather than lower it?

CHANGE 2 -- streamed valley splitting. splitIntegerTraces used to build EVERY input trace of a chunk
as an OpenMS MassTrace into `mts`, then call splitValleys(mts) -> `split` for the whole chunk, so the
chunk's input payload and its entire split output coexisted. Now it is per trace: build one
MassTrace from a REUSED vector<Peak2D> (was a per-trace std::list<Peak2D>), call
epd.detectPeaks(mt, split) with the single-trace overload, convert its children immediately, reuse
the buffers. ElutionPeakDetection is hoisted to CHUNK scope with its four setValue calls done once.
splitValleys() is deleted. The equivalence argument is: with width_filtering=off and
masstrace_snr_filtering=false the cross-trace routines are unreachable, and patches/
openms-epd-lockfree.patch makes the nested vector path literally
`for (i..) detectElutionPeaks_(mt_vec[i], out)` appending in order. VERIFY THAT ARGUMENT against the
actual OpenMS ElutionPeakDetection source and the patch. Specifically: does the single-trace
detectPeaks overload do anything the vector overload does not (it calls single_mtraces.clear()
first -- is that safe given I reuse the buffer)? Does chrom_fwhm/noise estimation depend on the
OTHER traces in the vector (e.g. a global noise or SNR estimate computed across mt_vec)? Is
findLocalExtrema or the smoothing affected by neighbours? Does MassTrace(vector) differ in any way
from MassTrace(list) -- ordering, size, or the internal centroid computation?

CHANGE 3 -- quantised fragment RT gate. In scoreCandidates_ the candidate loop over the IM slice
rejected 99.4% of visits with `fabs(frag_rt[fi] - pc.rt) > delta_rt`, reading an 8-byte double per
visit (867e9 visits = 6.9 TB per S30 run). I added a parallel `vector<uint16_t> q` holding the RT
quantised to buckets of width delta_rt (widened if the window RT span would overflow uint16), and a
2-byte reject `dq = q[fi] - bucketOf(pc.rt); if (dq < -2 || dq > 2) continue;` placed BEFORE the
unchanged exact test. Question: can a fragment that passes the exact test ever be rejected by the
quantised test? Consider bucket-width widening, negative RTs, precursors outside the window's
